            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house has alot of excess air leakage (and heat loss), mostly coming from:



** All around the furnace chimney as it passes through the ceiling



** The lower front edge of the furnace does not fit nicely against the ductwork it feeds below - alot of air leaks there



** The older metal sliding windows



** The older bathroom and kitchen range hood fan do not seal well &amp; should be replaced with new Energy Star rated units



** The wall access panel to the left of the clothes dryer has leakage from behind or under the bathtub on the other side



** The woodstove draws its' combustion air from inside the house (taking your nice warm inside air up &amp; out the chimney). That Princess wood stove should have an outside combustion air pipe brought in &amp; directly connected to the back of the stove



** The furnace heat supplies in the floor near the furnace (eg: the one in the kitchen floor) allows significant cold air up &amp; into the house. As discussed, I will email you the CMHC garbage bag air flow test so you can further investigate which sections of your heating ductwork are indeed giving you a problem. You can then share that information with your heating contractor to look further into it - either from inside the crawlspace or by feeding a small camera down the ductwork.



Savings: Based on the large amount of air leakage heat loss from your home, I have modeled a 40% reduction target, that is estimated to save $ 318 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 13.2 GJ.



If you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of the original 1976 house appear to be "shallow stud" construction with only R10 fibreglass batt insulation, and the exterior walls of the newer 1990 addition appear to be 2x6 construction with R20 fibreglass batt insulation. As per our discussions, you want to consider an exterior wall siding &amp; insulation upgrade so this would be a great time to add another minimum R15 of exterior wall insulation (behind the new siding).



I have modeled the addition of R15 (eg. 3" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the above foundation, exterior main floor walls of both the original 1976 house &amp; the 1990 addition - including the wall that adjoins the rear screened porch.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 341 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose to add a minimum R20, both your rebate &amp; energy savings will increase.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

With a significant snow &amp; ice buildup around your crawlspace walls, there was no access for me to see inside. I have assumed the crawlspace area is not directly heated by the furnace and it will have insulation in the top (ie: the floor of your house). There may be some insulation in the crawlspace walls. It's OK to have the crawlspace walls sealed up for the winter heating season, however there should be cross-ventilation through the crawlspace during the non-heating season to help eliminate any moisture and odours.



If you proceed to add 3" (R15) of rigid foam insulation to the upper exterior walls of the house, you may as well add the same R15 to the crawlspace walls.



Savings: By adding R15 insulation to the crawlspace walls, you could potentially save $ 83 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.5 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 1,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



</foundation>
                <windows>

There are 3 - 4 different types of windows in your house and none of them are current ENERGY STAR?????? rated. I decided to model an upgrade to all of the windows (includes the rear patio door) to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing all of the older windows &amp; the patio door with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 118 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,750 in rebates (up to 7 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for window upgrades.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</windows>
                <doors>

Both of the exterior doors in this house are older wooden doors that do not close well (and the rear door has a single pane of glass for a window). I recommend you replace these doors with new Energy Star steel insulated units with a minimum R value of 5.5, and at least double pane windows (triple is better) with low E coatings and argon gas fill between the panes.



Savings: By replacing these 2 older doors with higher efficiency Energy Star rated units (as outlined above), you could potentially save $ 83 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.4 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. As there has been an increase in houses &amp; businesses in Fort Smith using propane as a primary fuel source (regular delivery from Hay River), we discussed an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a Uniform Energy Factor of 0.91 or higher.



Although I quite like using electricity for heating &amp; hot water in "hydro electric" communities of the NWT (as there are no associated GHG's), I am often asked to illustrate the estimated cost of alternative fuels - as the homeowner looks for cost savings. Most Fort Smith residents are already using an alternative fuel for their primary heating appliance (oil or propane furnace or boiler), so performing this optional calculation for your hot water use is often requested. 



Savings: By replacing your current electric hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing propane-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.91 UEF, could potentially save $ 757 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an 83% efficient, direct vent oil furnace. As there has been an increase in houses &amp; businesses in Fort Smith using propane as a primary fuel source (regular delivery from Hay River), we discussed an option you may want to consider - have a look at an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. Your current furnace has ALOT of air leakage around the chimney up through the ceiling and also from where it mounts on the floor - this new furnace option would provide an opportunity to eliminate both of these air leakage situations.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 36 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



Yes ....... I realize the estimated fuel savings of only $36 sounds low. This is because fuel prices tend to have alot of fluctuation and fuel oil prices were quite low (compared to propane) when we completed our most recent Fuel Cost Library in December or 2020 (fuel rates for Fort Smith are summarized later in this report). If I used our previous 2019 fuel cost rates, the annual savings above would have been $ 836. The Arctic Energy Alliance is not promoting fuels based on their costs - we try to consider a balance between fuel costs, efficiency of the heating equipment available and the overall GHG reductions that can be achieved.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has a toilet that is an older unit that uses a lot of water &amp; it should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $13,750 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $5,000 for the main floor wall insulation

     - $1,000 for the crawlspace wall insulation

     - $1,750 for replacing 7 windows with new eligible units

     - $1,000 for a 30%+ air leakage reduction

     - $3,000 for an eligible furnace upgrade

     - $2,000 Bonus if you achieve a 20% - 30% total energy reduction 



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the crawlspace wall insulation and the furnace upgrade, however these would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - On-demand propane hot water heater - $750

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see section further down.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



VENTILATION



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - A kitchen range hood fan

        - 1 bathroom fans

        - A clothes dryer



While conducting the depressurization test (see info further down), the clothes dryer did not generate very much negative pressure, which makes me wonder if the vent pipe is blocked or has come away from the back of the dryer - you will need to check this. If it is blocked, this could result in a fire.



I would recommend you replace both the kitchen range hood &amp; the bathroom fan with new ENERGY STAR?????? rated units. For the bathroom fan, you may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood, bathroom fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of only 2.0 Pascals (see my comments in the ventilation section above about the clothes dryer). As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace is a direct vent unit, however the wood stove vents up a chimney &amp; draws it's combustion air from inside the house - so it could be susceptible to back-drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors, however did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.26 per kWh

Oil  - $0.90 per litre 

Propane - $0.73

Wood - $262.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.46 per bag, $299.62 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



